# SSH (Secure Shell)
- Used to remotely connect to another Linux machine
- Provides encrypted communication
- Syntax: ssh username@IP_address
- Requires:
  - IP address of target machine
  - Valid username and password


# Flags and Switches
- Commands accept arguments using flags
- Default command behavior can be modified
- Examples:
  - ls → list files
  - ls -a → show hidden files
  - ls -h → show options
  - man ls → manual page


# File and Directory Commands
- touch → create file
- mkdir → create directory
- rm → remove file
- rmdir → remove directory
- cp → copy files/folders
- mv → move/rename
- file → check file type


# File Types
- file filename → identify actual file type
- ASCII text supported


# Permissions
- Format: rwx rwx rwx
- First → owner
- Second → group
- Third → others

## Permission Values
- r → read (4)
- w → write (2)
- x → execute (1)

## Example
- rwxr-xr-x = 755

## Change Permission
- chmod 755 file


# Switching Users
- su username → switch user
- su - username → full login environment
- Requires password


# Important Directories
- /etc → system configuration files
- /var → variable data
- /root → root user home
- /boot → boot files
- /tmp → temporary files


# Networking Basics
- Network → system connection
- /etc/network/interfaces → network config

## Ports
- 80 → HTTP
- 443 → HTTPS

## Tools
- netstat → show network info
- ifconfig → network interface info


# Terminal Text Editors
- echo > and >> → not efficient for multi-line

## Editors
- nano (easy)
- vim (advanced)


# Nano Editor
- nano filename → open/create file

## Navigation
- Arrow keys → move
- Enter → next line

## Features
- Search text
- Copy & paste
- Jump to line number
- Show current line

## Shortcuts
- Ctrl + X → exit
- Ctrl + O → save
- Ctrl + W → search
- Ctrl + K → cut
- Ctrl + U → paste


# Vim Editor
- Advanced text editor
- Hard to learn but powerful

## Benefits
- Customizable shortcuts
- Syntax highlighting
- Works even if nano not installed
- Many tutorials available


